{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3784","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3784","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3784\",\"parent_revision\":\"86c2dd054ce029d73fd02f8c5e248fb2e2dca9a4\",\"root_revision\":\"b7d907f6b6a0096cafc4bc530f4020873edc47ef8e4c5e6a57b9dce9b4fa67df\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe rebase and receiving-pr-reviews skills must be consistently discoverable across supported harnesses, give agents clear and effective instructions, and operate with equal ease on GitHub and GitLab. Canonical skill content for both skills must live under .agents/skills, with Claude discovering each skill through a compatibility symlink under .claude/skills; receiving-pr-reviews already provides the conforming reference layout. Audit and improve both skills using the writing-for-agents guidance. Preserve existing GitHub support while adding equally easy GitLab operation, and evaluate that GitLab behavior against the user-provided repository at ~/repos/svs-gitlab.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-21T06:43:19.918283Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA SUMMARY\\n\\nGoal:\\n- Make rebase and receiving-pr-reviews canonically discoverable from .agents/skills, compliant with writing-for-agents guidance, and equally operable for GitHub pull requests and GitLab merge requests while preserving GitHub behavior.\\n\\nSuccess Output:\\n- A canonical .agents/skills directory and .claude/skills compatibility symlink for each skill, revised agent instructions, and tested forge-aware receiving-review commands whose GitLab read path is exercised from ~/repos/svs-gitlab without changing that repository.\\n\\nConditions (reverse prerequisites):\\n1. Authoritative scope | Requires: the backlog goal and named skill ecosystems | Why: prevents changes outside the two requested skills.\\n2. Canonical location convention | Requires: the repository pattern for .agents canonical directories and .claude compatibility symlinks | Why: establishes the exact relocation result.\\n3. Rebase relocation inventory | Requires: every tracked file and location reference in the rebase ecosystem | Why: moving an incomplete ecosystem or leaving stale paths breaks discovery.\\n4. Receiving-review relocation inventory | Requires: every tracked file, symlink, executable mode, and location reference in that ecosystem | Why: preserves an already-conforming layout.\\n5. Agent-writing standard | Requires: writing-for-agents and Skill Mechanics criteria | Why: the requested audit needs a concrete, repository-available standard.\\n6. Forge-specific surface inventory | Requires: current GitHub-only instructions, flags, API operations, models, tests, and fallback behavior | Why: GitLab parity cannot omit behavior that GitHub users retain.\\n7. Forge-neutral workflow contract | Requires: equivalent fetch, validate, reply, resolve, batch, watch, blocker, and repository-detection behaviors where each forge exposes them | Why: equal ease requires one workflow rather than separate manual procedures.\\n8. GitLab transport capability | Requires: supported GitLab CLI and API operations for host detection, pagination, merge-request discussions, replies, and resolution | Why: implementation must rest on available platform operations.\\n9. GitHub transport preservation | Requires: authenticated REST or GraphQL, pagination, and hostname selection remain supported | Why: the task forbids losing existing GitHub capabilities.\\n10. Evaluation target | Requires: the exact ~/repos/svs-gitlab repository, its GitLab remote, and a usable glab executable | Why: supplies a real self-managed GitLab discovery surface.\\n11. Verification coverage | Requires: existing receiving-review tests, new provider-specific tests, instruction validation, and read-only GitLab smoke checks | Why: parity and non-regression need observable evidence before commit.\\n12. Evaluation safety | Requires: preserve all existing changes in ~/repos/svs-gitlab and avoid unapproved external replies or resolutions | Why: the evaluation checkout is dirty and write operations have external side effects.\\n\\nVerification:\\n- Authoritative scope: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: backlog view for #3784 names only rebase, receiving-pr-reviews, the writing-for-agents pass, GitHub preservation, GitLab parity, and ~/repos/svs-gitlab.\\n- Canonical location convention: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: .tmp/scratch/reports/20260921-skill-relocation-read.md records four existing .claude/skills symlinks with targets of the form ../../.agents/skills/<name>.\\n- Rebase relocation inventory: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the relocation report and direct find show one file at .claude/skills/rebase/SKILL.md, no .agents counterpart, no auxiliary files, and no tracked external path reference.\\n- Receiving-review relocation inventory: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: direct find and the relocation report show the canonical .agents ecosystem, scripts, reference, executable modes, and .claude/skills/receiving-pr-reviews -> ../../.agents/skills/receiving-pr-reviews.\\n- Agent-writing standard: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: writing-for-agents/SKILL.md defines context pointers, information hierarchy, completion criteria, progressive disclosure, co-location, leading words, and pruning; SKILL-MECHANICS.md defines model invocation and frontmatter behavior.\\n- Forge-specific surface inventory: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: .agents/skills/receiving-pr-reviews/SKILL.md and its scripts expose GitHub-specific gh transport, --github override, PR terminology, GraphQL thread operations, GitHub MCP fallback, reply, resolve, batch, and watch behavior; .claude/skills/rebase/SKILL.md contains Git operations and no forge operation.\\n- Forge-neutral workflow contract: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the user requires equal GitLab ease without GitHub loss, while the existing skill defines the complete observable workflow; no contrary scope narrows parity to documentation only.\\n- GitLab transport capability: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: current official GitLab CLI documentation at https://docs.gitlab.com/cli/ documents GitLab.com, Self-Managed, and Dedicated support, git-remote host detection, and paginated lists; https://docs.gitlab.com/api/discussions/ documents merge-request discussion list, reply, and resolve operations.\\n- GitHub transport preservation: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: current official GitHub CLI documentation at https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_api documents authenticated REST and GraphQL, pagination, slurp, and hostname selection; the current scripts and tests provide the existing behavior baseline.\\n- Evaluation target: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: git -C ~/repos/svs-gitlab rev-parse returned /home/jamienelson/repos/svs-gitlab; origin is git@gitlab-au.aws.hivemindcloud.com:shieldai/hmv/svs.git; command -v glab returned /home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/glab and glab --version returned 1.118.0.\\n- Verification coverage: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the ecosystem includes test_pr_review_threads.py and direct CLI behavior; the exact GitLab repository and installed glab supply the live read surface, while write transports can be verified with provider-specific mocked command and response tests without external mutation.\\n- Evaluation safety: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: git status in ~/repos/svs-gitlab shows modified and untracked user files; the dispatch explicitly forbids mutating it, and repository policy requires preserving existing work.\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n\\nAssumptions to Confirm (EVIDENCE-DERIVED only):\\n- The current receiving-pr-reviews observable workflow is the parity inventory for GitLab wherever GitLab exposes an equivalent operation | Basis: the user requires equal ease and preservation of GitHub capabilities | Validation step: map every existing command and output signal to GitHub and GitLab tests before changing the implementation.\\n- Provider selection can be automatic from the current repository remote with an explicit override for non-current repositories | Basis: existing GitHub override behavior, official glab remote detection, and the GitLab evaluation repository remote | Validation step: add detection tests for GitHub, GitLab Self-Managed, explicit overrides, and ambiguous or absent remotes, then run a read-only detection smoke check in ~/repos/svs-gitlab.\\n\\nSafe Defaults Applied (SAFE-DEFAULTED only):\\n- Limit live evaluation in ~/repos/svs-gitlab to read-only host, repository, merge-request, and discussion discovery; verify reply and resolution transports with local tests | Why allowed: the scope is narrow, reversible, convention-following, observable before commit, preserves the dirty checkout, and creates no external side effect | Validation step: confirm git status is byte-for-byte unchanged after the read-only smoke checks and run provider-specific command construction and response parsing tests.\\n\\n<concerns>\\nEnvironmental configuration failure\\n  Observation: The first DH CLI invocation could not create a uv cache temporary file because the harness exposed /home/jamienelson/.cache/uv as read-only; the mandatory plan --help check and backlog view both succeeded after approved escalation.\\n  Why it matters: Subsequent backlog CLI operations in the same restricted harness need the approved uv execution route.\\n  Blocks current work: no\\n  Recommended owner/destination: harness environment\\n  Recommended follow-up: reuse the approved uv run route; no repository backlog item is warranted.\\n</concerns>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-22T02:35:31.467195Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA SUMMARY\\n\\nGoal:\\n- Make rebase and receiving-pr-reviews cross-harness and forge-portable, and make receiving-pr-reviews process the complete GitHub or GitLab review set through evidence-based holistic analysis and systemic resolution before any response or resolution.\\n\\nSuccess Output:\\n- Canonical cross-harness skill packages plus a tested provider-neutral review workflow that inventories all observable review inputs, clusters recurring or systemic concerns, enforces ordered analysis and implementation gates, preserves GitHub behavior, adds GitLab behavior, and ends every input and review cycle in an explicit verified state.\\n\\nConditions (reverse prerequisites):\\n1. Authoritative expanded scope | Requires: Description, Context, and Review Handling Intent from current #3784 | Why: the new review policy changes the required workflow, provider model, and verification surface.\\n2. Canonical package topology | Requires: both canonical skill directories under .agents/skills and relative Claude compatibility symlinks | Why: both revised skills must remain discoverable without duplicate bodies.\\n3. Agent-writing standard | Requires: writing-for-agents completion criteria, information hierarchy, condition-bearing pointers, co-location, and pruning | Why: the workflow must have one-way interpretation and observable bounds.\\n4. Complete review-input taxonomy | Requires: inline and top-level comments, questions, approvals, and rejections from human, bot, reviewer, and stakeholder actors | Why: omitted input classes invalidate the complete-set gate.\\n5. Provider review-state coverage | Requires: GitHub and GitLab mappings for every platform-observable input kind, actor, state, URL or stable reference, timestamps, revision context, and reply or resolution capability | Why: holistic assessment cannot operate on provider-specific omissions or invented equivalence.\\n6. Snapshot completeness | Requires: pagination and nested-comment completion, rejection of partial pages or mixed stale/fresh transport results, and explicit unavailable capabilities | Why: an incomplete snapshot can falsely hide patterns and terminal blockers.\\n7. Product and change-request goal context | Requires: repository instructions, stated PR or MR goal, changed scope, and current head revision | Why: each input must be judged against product improvement and intended change-request purpose.\\n8. Exhaustive per-input assessment | Requires: one recorded validity, relevance, evidence, affected scope, and disposition for every input | Why: every input must pass the policy before it can be acted on or closed.\\n9. Holistic pattern analysis | Requires: a complete input census and clusters based on shared invariant, affected component, root cause, requested outcome, or verification surface, including explicit singleton clusters | Why: recurring or systemic concerns must produce one coherent solution instead of repeated symptom patches.\\n10. Pre-action ordering gate | Requires: complete snapshot, every input assessed, every cluster recorded, unknowns resolved or escalated, and one coherent cluster-level plan before implementation, response, or resolution | Why: this is the enforceable boundary against knee-jerk patches.\\n11. Systemic implementation outcome | Requires: one class-level change and verification for accepted clusters, or an evidence-backed no-change, clarification, or superseded outcome | Why: every input needs a systemic outcome even when no code change is warranted.\\n12. Remote-before-response invariant | Requires: any warranted change verified and pushed to an inspectable revision before its response | Why: a reply or resolution must not outrun the evidence it cites.\\n13. Response and resolution capability | Requires: provider-correct inline reply, top-level response, resolution where supported, clarification-left-open behavior, and validated mutation responses | Why: assessed outcomes must be communicated and only then closed safely.\\n14. Per-input terminal states | Requires: observable states for accepted-and-verified, no-change-with-evidence, clarification-required, superseded, approval-assessed, rejection-addressed, response-posted, resolved, and provider-unresolvable inputs | Why: every review input needs a checkable end state without pretending all provider objects are resolvable.\\n15. Review-cycle terminal states | Requires: action required, clarification pending, complete-set plan ready, implementation verification failed, reviewability blocked, approved, quiet window elapsed, and transport or schema error outcomes | Why: the workflow must stop or loop deterministically.\\n16. GitHub preservation | Requires: current commands, pagination, top-level response matching, exact Codex identity and revision checks, MCP whole-snapshot fallback, and current GitHub fields or an explicitly approved compatibility transition | Why: GitLab support cannot remove existing GitHub capability.\\n17. GitLab parity without fabricated equivalence | Requires: custom-host detection, discussions and notes, approvals, diff versions, reply, resolve, top-level comments, unavailable approval semantics, and read-only validation | Why: equal ease requires native GitLab operation while platform gaps remain explicit.\\n18. Verification coverage | Requires: fixtures for every input type and actor, complete-set blocking, cluster formation, systemic one-change handling, ordering violations, mutation validation, terminal states, and cross-provider parity | Why: prose alone cannot prove that symptom patches and premature responses are prevented.\\n19. Evaluation safety | Requires: read-only live checks in ~/repos/svs-gitlab and local fixture coverage for mutations | Why: the supplied checkout contains user changes and remote review mutations are external side effects.\\n20. Public approval and rejection contract | Requires: the chosen JSON representation and terminal semantics when GitHub has a current Codex signal but GitLab has no evidenced equivalent | Why: the provider-neutral CLI cannot implement truthful compatible output without a product-contract decision.\\n\\nVerification:\\n- Authoritative expanded scope: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: current backlog view exposes one Review Handling Intent entry requiring all named input types, complete-set assessment, systemic clustering, and the assessment-to-response sequence.\\n- Canonical package topology: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: 20260922-cross-harness-skill-audit.md inventories the canonical .agents roots, the existing receiving-pr-reviews symlink, and the exact rebase relocation required.\\n- Agent-writing standard: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: 20260922-receiving-writing-audit.md and 20260922-rebase-writing-audit.md apply writing-for-agents and define the needed completion, disclosure, and terminal-state criteria.\\n- Complete review-input taxonomy: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent explicitly names inline and top-level comments, questions, approvals, rejections, humans, bots, reviewers, and stakeholders.\\n- Provider review-state coverage: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the GitHub audit already fetches threads, submitted reviews, PR comments, reactions, actor, revision, and reviewability; the GitLab audit establishes discussions, notes, approval data, actor data, MR state, and diff versions. The expanded intent requires normalizing observable states and marking provider-unavailable equivalents rather than inventing them; no source contradicts that mapping.\\n- Snapshot completeness: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the forge and writing audits require full pagination, nested-comment completion, rejection of failed later pages and partial MCP snapshots, and one transport per snapshot.\\n- Product and change-request goal context: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent names product improvement and intended PR or MR task as the assessment criteria; repository instructions and current change-request metadata are existing inputs.\\n- Exhaustive per-input assessment: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent says every input must pass assessment and discovery; the receiving audit supplies evidence-bearing dispositions and requires exactly one disposition per fetched item.\\n- Holistic pattern analysis: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent explicitly requires assessing the complete set, identifying recurring, related, or systemic patterns, and addressing them holistically.\\n- Pre-action ordering gate: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent explicitly places assessment, discovery, planning, and systemic implementation before response or resolution and forbids acting before complete-set analysis.\\n- Systemic implementation outcome: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent requires a systemic change when warranted and defines an evidence-backed no-change conclusion as the systemic outcome otherwise.\\n- Remote-before-response invariant: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: 20260922-receiving-writing-audit.md identifies push-before-response as the strongest existing invariant and requires its preservation.\\n- Response and resolution capability: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: existing GitHub commands and official GitLab discussion operations support reply and resolution; the audits define combined ordering, clarification, top-level comments, and strict mutation response validation.\\n- Per-input terminal states: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: explicit input types, evidence-bearing dispositions, provider capability differences, and the required response sequence force distinct resolved, open-clarification, no-change, approval, rejection, and provider-unresolvable outcomes; treating every object as a resolvable thread contradicts provider evidence.\\n- Review-cycle terminal states: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the receiving audit defines ACTION_REQUIRED, APPROVED, QUIET_WINDOW_ELAPSED, BLOCKED, and ERROR; the expanded ordering gate additionally requires plan-ready, clarification-pending, and verification-failed states before response.\\n- GitHub preservation: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: forge audit and implementation design inventory all current GitHub reads, commands, fields, exact Codex rule, response matching, mutation paths, and MCP fallback.\\n- GitLab parity without fabricated equivalence: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: forge audit records the GitLab API and glab surfaces, the self-managed evaluation repository, and live approved=true with zero required approvals and no approvers, proving generic approval cannot represent Codex approval.\\n- Verification coverage: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: existing audits supply provider and ordering tests; Review Handling Intent forces added fixtures that fail on incomplete census, premature implementation or response, unclustered related inputs, repeated symptom fixes, and missing evidence-backed terminals.\\n- Evaluation safety: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: existing audit evidence records a dirty ~/repos/svs-gitlab worktree and limits live validation to GET-equivalent detection, fetch, and zero-window watch operations.\\n- Public approval and rejection contract: Evidence=UNRESOLVED | Disposition=REQUIRES-USER | Basis: 20260922-skill-implementation-plan.md records three materially different public schemas; the recommended option widens an existing field type, the provider-discriminated option creates two schemas, and the internal-only option hides unavailable versus not-approved. No user instruction or repository rule selects among these public behaviors.\\n\\nDecision: BLOCKED\\n\\nMissing Inputs Requested:\\n\\nPublic review-state contract:\\n- Question: Which public JSON and terminal contract should represent approvals and rejections across providers: A) one explicit provider-neutral approval_signal and review-input state, retaining codex_approved for GitHub and using null for unavailable GitLab equivalence; B) preserve GitHub JSON byte-for-byte and use a provider-discriminated GitLab schema; or C) keep the tri-state internal and expose only provider-specific watch stop reasons?\\n- Why it matters: the choice changes agent-facing JSON compatibility, whether unavailable can be distinguished from not approved, and when APPROVED is a truthful terminal.\\n- Known constraints: GitHub has an exact current-revision Codex +1 convention; GitLab generic approved=true can mean zero approvals are required; Review Handling Intent requires approvals and rejections to be assessed as inputs; no GitLab Codex equivalent is evidenced.\\n- Options under current constraints: A is truthful and uniform but widens the codex_approved type; B preserves GitHub output but creates two schemas; C avoids a schema change but hides a material state and weakens equal ease.\\n- Risk if wrong: existing GitHub consumers can break, GitLab can report a false approval, or the workflow can terminate without exposing why approval is unavailable.\\n\\n<concerns>\\nStale planning artifact\\n  Observation: 20260922-skill-implementation-plan.md classifies each actionable item before implementation but does not require complete-set clustering before any action and treats approval primarily as a completion signal.\\n  Why it matters: executing that plan unchanged would violate the later Review Handling Intent section.\\n  Blocks current work: yes, until the public review-state contract is chosen and the plan is revised after RT-ICA approval.\\n  Recommended owner/destination: #3784 planning stream\\n  Recommended follow-up: revise the workflow contract, provider models, task graph, tests, and terminal states after the clarification is answered.\\n</concerns>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-22T03:59:03.014647Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA SUMMARY\\n\\nGoal:\\n- Make rebase and receiving-pr-reviews cross-harness and forge-portable, and make receiving-pr-reviews process the complete GitHub or GitLab review set through evidence-based holistic analysis and systemic resolution before REVIEW_COMPLETE.\\n\\nSuccess Output:\\n- Canonical cross-harness skill packages plus a tested provider-neutral review workflow that emits one normalized review_inputs[] schema, inventories and clusters all observable review inputs, preserves GitHub behavior, adds truthful GitLab behavior, enforces the approved ordering gates, and emits REVIEW_COMPLETE only after re-checking the provider.\\n\\nConditions (reverse prerequisites):\\n1. Authoritative expanded scope | Requires: Description, Context, Review Handling Intent, and Public Review State Contract from current #3784 | Why: these sections jointly define packaging, forge parity, review policy, schema, and completion semantics.\\n2. Canonical package topology | Requires: both canonical skill directories under .agents/skills and relative Claude compatibility symlinks | Why: both revised skills must remain discoverable without duplicate bodies.\\n3. Agent-writing standard | Requires: writing-for-agents completion criteria, information hierarchy, condition-bearing pointers, co-location, and pruning | Why: the workflow must have one-way interpretation and observable bounds.\\n4. Complete normalized review-input taxonomy | Requires: one review_inputs[] schema covering inline and top-level comments, questions, approvals, and rejections from human, bot, reviewer, and stakeholder actors | Why: omitted input classes invalidate the complete-set gate.\\n5. Provider review-state coverage | Requires: GitHub and GitLab mappings for every platform-observable input kind, actor, state, URL or stable reference, timestamps, revision context, and reply or resolution capability | Why: holistic assessment cannot operate on provider-specific omissions or invented equivalence.\\n6. Snapshot completeness | Requires: pagination and nested-comment completion, rejection of partial pages or mixed stale/fresh transport results, and explicit unavailable capabilities | Why: an incomplete snapshot can falsely hide patterns and terminal blockers.\\n7. Product and change-request goal context | Requires: repository instructions, stated PR or MR goal, changed scope, and current head revision | Why: each input must be judged against product improvement and intended change-request purpose.\\n8. Exhaustive per-input assessment | Requires: one recorded validity, relevance, evidence, affected scope, and disposition for every normalized input | Why: every input must pass the policy before it can be acted on or closed.\\n9. Holistic pattern analysis | Requires: a complete input census and clusters based on shared invariant, affected component, root cause, requested outcome, or verification surface, including explicit singleton clusters | Why: recurring or systemic concerns must produce one coherent solution instead of repeated symptom patches.\\n10. Pre-action ordering gate | Requires: complete snapshot, every input assessed, every cluster recorded, unknowns resolved or escalated, and one coherent cluster-level plan before implementation, response, or resolution | Why: this is the enforceable boundary against knee-jerk patches.\\n11. Systemic implementation outcome | Requires: one class-level change and verification for accepted clusters, or an evidence-backed no-change, clarification, or superseded outcome | Why: every input needs a systemic outcome even when no code change is warranted.\\n12. Remote-before-response invariant | Requires: any warranted change verified and pushed to an inspectable revision before its response | Why: a reply or resolution must not outrun the evidence it cites.\\n13. Response and resolution capability | Requires: provider-correct inline reply, top-level response, resolution where supported, clarification-left-open behavior, and validated mutation responses | Why: assessed outcomes must be communicated and only then closed safely.\\n14. Per-input terminal states | Requires: observable states for accepted-and-verified, no-change-with-evidence, clarification-required, superseded, approval-assessed, rejection-addressed, response-posted, resolved, and provider-unresolvable inputs | Why: every review input needs a checkable end state without pretending all provider objects are resolvable.\\n15. REVIEW_COMPLETE terminal gate | Requires: complete-set assessment, pattern clustering, systemic action or evidenced no-change, verification, communication, and a provider re-check showing no unresolved or newly arrived input | Why: the approved contract forbids approval, rejection, or an earlier snapshot from completing the workflow.\\n16. GitHub preservation | Requires: current commands, pagination, top-level response matching, exact Codex identity and revision checks, MCP whole-snapshot fallback, existing codex_approved behavior, and compatible current fields | Why: GitLab support cannot remove existing GitHub capability.\\n17. GitLab parity without fabricated equivalence | Requires: custom-host detection, discussions and notes, approvals, diff versions, reply, resolve, top-level comments, unavailable-or-null Codex equivalence, and read-only validation | Why: equal ease requires native GitLab operation while platform gaps remain explicit.\\n18. Verification coverage | Requires: fixtures for every input type and actor, complete-set blocking, cluster formation, systemic one-change handling, ordering violations, mutation validation, unavailable GitLab approval, provider re-check, and cross-provider parity | Why: prose alone cannot prove that symptom patches and premature REVIEW_COMPLETE are prevented.\\n19. Evaluation safety | Requires: read-only live checks in ~/repos/svs-gitlab and local fixture coverage for mutations | Why: the supplied checkout contains user changes and remote review mutations are external side effects.\\n20. Public review-state contract | Requires: normalized output, compatibility rule, unavailable semantics, approval and rejection treatment, and completion terminal | Why: these are agent-facing product-contract choices and must be explicit before implementation.\\n\\nVerification:\\n- Authoritative expanded scope: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: current backlog view exposes one entry each for Review Handling Intent and Public Review State Contract alongside the original Description and Context.\\n- Canonical package topology: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: 20260922-cross-harness-skill-audit.md inventories the canonical .agents roots, the existing receiving-pr-reviews symlink, and the exact rebase relocation required.\\n- Agent-writing standard: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: 20260922-receiving-writing-audit.md and 20260922-rebase-writing-audit.md apply writing-for-agents and define the needed completion, disclosure, and terminal-state criteria.\\n- Complete normalized review-input taxonomy: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Public Review State Contract explicitly selects one review_inputs[] schema for inline comments, top-level comments, questions, approvals, and rejections across providers; Review Handling Intent supplies the actor categories.\\n- Provider review-state coverage: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the GitHub audit fetches threads, submitted reviews, PR comments, reactions, actor, revision, and reviewability; the GitLab audit establishes discussions, notes, approval data, actor data, MR state, and diff versions. The approved schema requires normalizing observable states and explicitly marking unavailable equivalents.\\n- Snapshot completeness: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the forge and writing audits require full pagination, nested-comment completion, rejection of failed later pages and partial MCP snapshots, and one transport per snapshot.\\n- Product and change-request goal context: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent names product improvement and intended PR or MR task as the assessment criteria; repository instructions and current change-request metadata are existing inputs.\\n- Exhaustive per-input assessment: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent requires every input to pass assessment and discovery; Public Review State Contract explicitly keeps approvals and rejections inside review_inputs[] and denies them shortcut behavior.\\n- Holistic pattern analysis: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent requires complete-set clustering; Public Review State Contract repeats clustering and systemic pattern identification as a REVIEW_COMPLETE prerequisite.\\n- Pre-action ordering gate: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Review Handling Intent places assessment, discovery, planning, and systemic implementation before response or resolution; Public Review State Contract preserves complete-set assessment and clustering before completion.\\n- Systemic implementation outcome: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: both approved sections require systemic resolution when warranted and an evidence-backed no-change disposition otherwise.\\n- Remote-before-response invariant: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: 20260922-receiving-writing-audit.md identifies push-before-response as the strongest existing invariant and requires its preservation.\\n- Response and resolution capability: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: existing GitHub commands and official GitLab discussion operations support reply and resolution; the audits define combined ordering, clarification, top-level comments, and strict mutation response validation.\\n- Per-input terminal states: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: normalized input types, evidence-bearing dispositions, provider capability differences, and the ordered communication requirement force distinct resolved, open-clarification, no-change, approval, rejection, and provider-unresolvable outcomes.\\n- REVIEW_COMPLETE terminal gate: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: Public Review State Contract enumerates all six completion conditions and explicitly prohibits emission before every condition is satisfied.\\n- GitHub preservation: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: forge audit inventories current GitHub reads, commands, fields, exact Codex rule, response matching, mutations, and MCP fallback; the approved contract explicitly retains codex_approved.\\n- GitLab parity without fabricated equivalence: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: forge audit records the GitLab surfaces and live approved=true with zero required approvals and no approvers; the approved contract requires unavailable or null when no equivalent Codex signal exists.\\n- Verification coverage: Evidence=EVIDENCE-DERIVED | Disposition=N/A | Basis: existing audits supply provider and ordering tests; the approved contract forces added assertions for normalized approvals and rejections, unavailable GitLab equivalence, complete-set clustering, communication, provider re-check, and no premature REVIEW_COMPLETE.\\n- Evaluation safety: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: audit evidence records a dirty ~/repos/svs-gitlab worktree and limits live validation to GET-equivalent detection, fetch, and zero-window watch operations.\\n- Public review-state contract: Evidence=AVAILABLE | Disposition=N/A | Basis: the user-approved Public Review State Contract selects one normalized review_inputs[] schema, retains GitHub codex_approved, requires GitLab unavailable or null without an equivalent, makes approvals and rejections assessed inputs, and defines REVIEW_COMPLETE.\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n\\nAssumptions to Confirm (EVIDENCE-DERIVED only):\\n- Provider normalization can represent every platform-observable review input in the approved review_inputs[] schema while retaining provider-specific identifiers and capabilities | Basis: both provider audits expose the required source data, and unavailable equivalence is now explicitly permitted | Validation step: run schema fixtures for every input kind and actor on both providers, including missing GitLab Codex equivalence.\\n- Per-input terminal states are represented separately from the REVIEW_COMPLETE cycle terminal | Basis: the contract requires approvals and rejections to remain inputs and forbids any one input from shortcutting completion | Validation step: assert an individually assessed approval or rejection cannot emit REVIEW_COMPLETE while any input, communication, or provider re-check remains incomplete.\\n\\nSafe Defaults Applied (SAFE-DEFAULTED only):\\n- Limit live evaluation in ~/repos/svs-gitlab to read-only host, repository, merge-request, and review-state discovery; verify reply, resolution, and communication transports with local tests | Why allowed: the scope is narrow, reversible, convention-following, observable before commit, preserves the dirty checkout, and creates no external side effect | Validation step: confirm pre/post repository fingerprints match and run provider-specific mutation and REVIEW_COMPLETE fixtures locally.\\n\\n<concerns>\\nStale planning artifact\\n  Observation: 20260922-skill-implementation-plan.md predates Review Handling Intent and Public Review State Contract, so its workflow and tests do not fully encode review_inputs[], complete-set clustering, or the approved REVIEW_COMPLETE gate.\\n  Why it matters: executing that plan unchanged would omit current approved requirements.\\n  Blocks current work: no; the implementation gate is complete, but planning must be revised before source implementation.\\n  Recommended owner/destination: #3784 planning stream\\n  Recommended follow-up: revise the workflow contract, provider models, task graph, acceptance criteria, tests, and terminal states from this current RT-ICA.\\n</concerns>\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: User request in current session\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Review Handling Intent\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-22T02:14:41.584052Z</sub>\\n\\nReview input can arrive as inline or top-level comments, questions, approvals, or rejections from humans, bots, reviewers, and stakeholders.\\n\\nEvery input must be assessed under the goals of improving the product and clearly communicating the intended task of the pull request or merge request.\\n\\nThe workflow must assess the complete review set before acting, identify recurring, related, or systemic patterns across all inputs, and address those patterns holistically. It must not defer systemic concerns into repeated symptom fixes in later commits.\\n\\nNo input may trigger a knee-jerk patch. Before any response or resolution, every input must pass through assessment, discovery, planning, and systemic implementation. This sequence must establish the input's validity and relevance, gather evidence and affected scope, plan a coherent response, and implement the systemic change when action is warranted. When assessment finds no implementation change is warranted, that conclusion and its evidence are the systemic outcome to communicate before resolving the input.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Public Review State Contract\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-22T03:31:57.948404Z</sub>\\n\\nOne normalized review_inputs[] schema represents every review input across providers, including inline comments, top-level comments, questions, approvals, and rejections.\\n\\nGitHub retains its existing codex_approved signal. When no equivalent Codex approval signal is available on GitLab, GitLab must represent that state explicitly as unavailable or null. The workflow must not fabricate, infer, or substitute a Codex approval.\\n\\nApprovals and rejections remain entries in review_inputs[]. Neither is a completion shortcut, and neither may bypass assessment of the complete review set.\\n\\nREVIEW_COMPLETE is valid only after all of the following are complete:\\n\\n1. Assess the complete set of normalized review inputs across the provider.\\n2. Cluster related inputs and identify recurring or systemic patterns across the complete set.\\n3. Resolve warranted concerns systemically, or record an evidence-backed no-change disposition when implementation is not warranted.\\n4. Verify the resulting implementation or no-change disposition against the intended task and product outcome.\\n5. Communicate the assessment, action, evidence, and outcome to the relevant review inputs and stakeholders.\\n6. Re-check the provider after communication to confirm that no unresolved or newly arrived review input remains.\\n\\nREVIEW_COMPLETE must not be emitted before every requirement above is satisfied.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"00119362557d600f812d368ae6a09d1104a1c750c0a4dc9a90667097e7d8b635\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABV_qGOQ\"}"}